## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design automated degradation with controlled risk during external dependency pressure and multi-region operation.

## Key Concepts
- Circuit breakers and failover
- Per-region degradation strategies
- Idempotent queues and exactly-once delivery
- Canary and synthetic testing
- Rollback criteria and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for circuit breaker state machine
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold, resetTime) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.resetTime = resetTime;
    this.failures = 0;
    this.state = 'CLOSED';
  }
  onFailure() {
    this.failures++;
    if (this.failures > this.threshold) this.state = 'OPEN';
  }
  canRequest() { return this.state !== 'OPEN'; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant services with mixed SLIs?
- How would you validate degradation paths in canaries without impacting customers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical backpressure control, multi-region rollout, observability, and safe rollback with concrete metrics.

## Key Concepts

- Backpressure and queueing signals
- Auto-tuning concurrency via closed-loop control
- Circuit breakers and safe rollouts
- Cross-region canaries and feature flags
- Observability: p95 latency, backlog, error rate

## Code Example

```javascript
// Adaptive limiter sketch
class AdaptiveLimiter {
  constructor(targetP95 = 300, maxConns = 200) {
    this.targetP95 = targetP95;
    this.maxConns = maxConns;
    this.currConns = Math.floor(maxConns * 0.6);
  }
  onMetrics(p95, backlog) {
    // simple proportional rule; can be replaced by PID
    if (p95 < this.targetP95 && backlog < 0.8 * 5000) {
      this.currConns = Math.min(this.currConns + 5, this.maxConns);
    } else {
      this.currConns = Math.max(this.currConns - 5, 1);
    }
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the controller with synthetic load and canaries?
- How do you account for clock skew and regional differences in latency metrics?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical debugging in a real-world, multi-region environment with tenant isolation. Emphasizes telemetry-driven root-cause analysis, dynamic traffic shaping, and controlled validation before rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Telemetry-driven debugging across regions
- Per-tenant isolation and rate limiting
- Cache partitioning and traffic shifting
- Progressive degradation with safe fallbacks
- Canary and synthetic test-based validation
- Rollback criteria tied to SLOs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rate limiter sketch for per-tenant edge gateway
class RateLimiter {
  constructor(limitPerTenant) { this.limit = limitPerTenant; this.map = new Map(); }
  allow(tenantId) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const rec = this.map.get(tenantId) || { t: now, c: 0 };
    if (now - rec.t > 1000) { rec.t = now; rec.c = 0; }
    if (rec.c < this.limit) { rec.c++; this.map.set(tenantId, rec); return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success and determine completion of the degradation plan?
- What metrics and traces would you surface to stakeholders during the rollback decision?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manage tenant-level reliability with shared budgets and fast containment.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLOs and error budgets
- Tenant-scoped routing and feature flags
- Tenant-level telemetry and alerting
- Safe rollback and RCA

## Code Example
```javascript
// tenant routing middleware sketch
function tenantRouter(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers["x-tenant-id"];
  const degraded = global.degradedTenants?.has(tenant);
  if (degraded) req.degraded = true;
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate detection of affected tenants during outages?
- How do you prevent cascading alerts while preserving SLA guarantees?
